# Chrome flags for running the ChromeOS UI on the NVIDIA GPU (Stage 3).
#
# Append to /etc/chrome_dev.conf on the device, then restart the UI:
#   restart ui
#
# /etc/chrome_dev.conf only exists on dev/test images, which is one more reason the
# board builds with --no-enable-rootfs-verification.
#
# Why this file is needed at all: ChromeOS's graphics stack assumes Mesa. Chrome's
# Ozone/DRM backend allocates through minigbm and gets EGL from Mesa's libEGL. NVIDIA
# ships its own EGL implementation plus a separate GBM backend (libnvidia-egl-gbm), so
# Chrome has to be pointed at them explicitly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ozone platform. DRM/GBM is already the ChromeOS default; stated explicitly because
# the whole point of this stage is which DRM device gets used.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--ozone-platform=drm

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Force GPU-accelerated compositing. If the NVIDIA path fails, Chrome will silently
# fall back to software rendering and the UI will appear to "work" while proving
# nothing. These flags make a failure visible instead of quiet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--ignore-gpu-blocklist
--enable-gpu-rasterization
--disable-gpu-driver-bug-workarounds

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --use-gl=egl / --use-angle=gl-egl are a poor fit for Ash GBM (native EGL).
# Applied flags live in chrome_dev.conf.display (nvidia-generic-auth).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vulkan. Optional for the UI, but turning it on here is the cheapest end-to-end check
# that the NVIDIA ICD is being found by something other than our own test binary.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--enable-features=Vulkan
--use-vulkan=native

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logging. When the display path breaks the screen is exactly the wrong place to look
# for the reason, so be verbose and expect to read this over SSH.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--enable-logging=stderr
--v=1
--vmodule=*ozone*=2,*gbm*=2,*drm*=2,*gl_*=2
